\section*{Overview}

The SIM-QSTATE-ASTRO-v9.1-FINAL-INTEGRATED engine incorporates a unified 
multi-messenger ingestion pipeline capable of processing heterogeneous 
astrophysical telemetry streams in real time. This pipeline integrates 
gravitational wave strain profiles, astrometric 6D vectors, and synthetic 
cosmological data into a coherent GPU-accelerated manifold. Each telemetry 
type is cryptographically validated, normalized, and bound to the relativistic 
metric grid before entering the CUDA compute core.

This chapter describes the ingestion architecture, data structures, 
cryptographic safeguards, asynchronous transfer mechanisms, and runtime 
autonomy that enable continuous multi-source observational processing.

\section{Telemetry Modalities}

\subsection{LIGO Gravitational Wave Strain}

The engine supports ingestion of transverse-traceless gravitational wave 
signals through the \texttt{LIGOWaveTelemetry} structure:

\begin{quote}
``double h\_plus; \quad double h\_cross;''
\end{quote}

These strain components are used to perturb the spacetime metric via the 
tensor projection kernel described in Chapter~\ref{ch:system_architecture}. 
Incoming strain values are scaled by astrometric velocity magnitudes to 
produce grid-wide perturbation fields.

\subsection{Gaia DR3 Astrometric 6D Vectors}

Astrometric telemetry is represented through the \texttt{Gaia6DTelemetry} 
structure:

\begin{quote}
``double x, y, z; \quad double vx, vy, vz;''
\end{quote}

These vectors provide positional and velocity information for stellar 
objects. Velocity components modulate gravitational wave perturbation 
amplitudes, enabling dynamic coupling between LIGO strain and Gaia motion.

